over the past decade chinese president  xi jinping and russian president  vladimir putin have become close  they made pancakes together  and toasted with vodka  she even presented china's first  friendship medal to putin the alignment  between the two countries is getting  closer and some analysts would say  it's more close than at any point in the  past 60 years  but that relationship is now getting  tested after russia's invasion of  ukraine which has prompted the u.s and  its allies to drop a barrage of  sanctions against moscow  they included hitting russia's energy  industry a major source of the country's  income as well as unplugging the world's  11th largest economy from the global  financial system  but china has taken a different position  as russia's biggest trading partner  china could provide the country with  much needed help by purchasing energy  and providing financial services but the  friendship has limits  one of the biggest hits to russia has  been its energy sector it's the world's  largest natural gas exporter and  produces about 10 percent of the world's  oil and last year total exports reached  about 240 billion dollars  so when putin deployed troops to  breakaway regions of ukraine the west  tried to hit russia where it could hurt  one of the responses that the west has  had to russia's invasion is to  re-examine the energy relationship it  has with russia one of the biggest  re-examinations was the nordstream 2  project a newly built pipeline that was  expected to send 55 billion cubic meters  of natural gas from russia directly to  germany doubling the volume germany said  it would halt the 11 billion project  and as russia has continued bombarding  civilian areas of ukraine the us took  action against russian energy we're  banning all imports of russian oil and  gas and energy  the uk also said it would phase out  russian oil imports while the eu said  it's aiming to cut some imports too  china could help fill some of the gaps  a few weeks before russia's invasion  putin visited the chinese president  ahead of the winter olympics during  their meeting beijing agreed to buy 100  million tons of crude oil from its  neighbor there was another agreement  between russia and china to construct a  new pipeline that would send an  additional 10 billion cubic meters of  natural gas that's on top of an existing  1800 mile pipeline that's already  sending 16 billion cubic meters of  natural gas per year to china but even  with all the planned pipelines running  at full capacity china's purchase would  still fall short of what the nordstream  2 pipeline was expected to send each  year to germany and all of these chinese  projects will take years to fully bear  fruit the deals are significant in size  but they still don't amount to any sort  of immediate economic lifeline for  russia the new pipeline won't be  constructed until 2026 and even the  crude oil deal that will stretch out  over the course of a decade  energy hasn't been the only sector hit  by the west it's also been isolating  russia's financial industry the u.s and  its allies said they would remove seven  russian banks from the global financial  messaging system swift which is used by  more than 11 000 banks and other  organizations in more than 200 countries  and territories and getting excluded  means these banks can't execute  financial transactions meet obligations  or receive payments for exports  practically disconnecting a large part  of russia's economy but china could help  out here too there was speculation that  perhaps china's rival system the  cross-border inter-bank payment system  could provide something of a lifeline to  russian banks  however the chinese system has its own  limitations last year it handled an  average of just 13 000 transactions a  day although the comparison isn't exact  swift processed more than 40 million  messages per day china's system is teeny  at the moment so analysts don't really  think that the chinese system is  um a viable alternative  russian banks could also face problems  with their credit cards visa and  mastercard said they're suspending their  russia operations which practically  blocks many russians from purchasing  goods or services outside the country to  get around this several russian banks  said they were working on the  possibility of issuing cards that used  china's state-owned union pay system  these cards are accepted widely online  and in stores around the world but  switching to this new system would also  take time  there are ways for china to help russia  economically but every time china helps  russia  it risks provoking for the confrontation  with the west  so far china has been trying to strike a  delicate balance it has opposed the  western sanctions  but at the same time it hasn't signaled  that it would be ready to backstop  russia's economy u.s officials said that  russia has also asked china for military  equipment and the white house issued a  warning should they provide military or  other assistance that of course violates  sanctions or  or supports the war effort that there  will be significant consequences  the chinese embassy in the u.s says it  has never heard of such a request from  moscow and china has earlier made vague  pledges to facilitate negotiations to  end the war  the bottom line for china is to ensure  its own strategic interests beijing  wants to keep russia close as a  strategic collaborator against the west  but it's also having to manage its own  relationship with the us and west to  keep that from going off the rails
 

